ABSTRACT = r"""Instruction-following image generators can rewrite a single price, date or phone number inside a scanned receipt so convincingly that human inspectors and off-the-shelf forensic detectors perform near chance. We study this threat on AIForge-Doc v2, a paired benchmark of 3,066 GPT-Image-2 field forgeries with pixel-aligned authentic originals and tampering masks. We remove 31 duplicated source documents (6 of which leak across the official train/test split) and score both versions of every test document. Because the forged field covers a median of only 0.76\% of the page, page-level recipes fail: raw-pixel logistic regression scores AUC <<f3(M['pixel_lr']['auc'])>><<(' and a fine-tuned ResNet-50 on resized pages ' + f3(M['global_cnn']['auc'])) if 'global_cnn' in M else ''>>. We propose FieldForensics, a \emph{localize-to-detect} U-Net that fuses RGB with an SRM noise residual at native resolution, is trained on field-centred crops with \emph{paired hard negatives}, and scores a page by the peak of its tampering map. On 1,212 held-out images it reaches an AUC of <<f3(AUC)>> (95\% CI <<ci(m0['auc_ci'])>>), a paired two-alternative forced-choice accuracy of <<pc(m0['pair_acc'])>>\% and a pixel F1 of <<f3(m0['pix_f1'])>>, against published zero-shot AUCs of 0.585--0.599 for DocTamper and TruFor and 0.501 for humans. Block-wise compression statistics (ELA, noise, blockiness) are a strong baseline (AUC <<f3(M['forensic_gbdt']['auc'])>>) because they exploit the compression-history mismatch of the pasted field. The clean-trained network learns this cue by itself, whereas a variant trained with degradation augmentation (FieldForensics-R, <<f3(AUCR)>>) suppresses it and recovers it only through fusion with the statistics (<<f3(M['fusion']['auc']) if 'fusion' in M else '--'>>).<<(' Under JPEG-75 re-compression the statistics fall to chance (%.3f), FieldForensics to %.3f and FieldForensics-R to %.3f, which exposes a trade-off between clean accuracy and robustness.' % (R['robust']['forensic_gbdt']['jpeg75']['auc'], R['robust'][PK]['jpeg75']['auc'], R['robust']['main_s0']['jpeg75']['auc'])) if all('jpeg75' in R.get('robust', {}).get(k, {}) for k in ('forensic_gbdt', PK, 'main_s0')) else ''>>"""
